      <fo:block id="id587974">
        <fo:block font-family="Helvetica" font-weight="bold" keep-with-next.within-column="always"
          space-before.minimum="0.8em" space-before.optimum="1.0em" space-before.maximum="1.2em"
          text-align="start" start-indent="0pt">
          <fo:marker marker-class-name="section.head.marker">References</fo:marker>
          <fo:block font-size="12pt" font-weight="bold">References</fo:block>
        </fo:block>
      </fo:block>
      <fo:block start-indent="0pt">
        <fo:block id="id588005" keep-together.within-column="auto" space-before.minimum="0.5em"
          space-before.optimum="1em" space-before.maximum="2em" space-after.minimum="0.5em"
          space-after.optimum="1em" space-after.maximum="2em">
          <fo:block font-style="italic" font-size="10pt" font-weight="normal" text-align="center"
            hyphenate="false" space-after.minimum="0.4em" space-after.optimum="0.6em"
            space-after.maximum="0.8em" space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
            space-before.maximum="12pt" keep-with-next.within-column="always">Table 1  References</fo:block>
        </fo:block>
      </fo:block>
      <fo:block id="id588046">
        <fo:block font-family="Helvetica" font-weight="bold" keep-with-next.within-column="always"
          space-before.minimum="0.8em" space-before.optimum="1.0em" space-before.maximum="1.2em"
          text-align="start" start-indent="0pt">
          <fo:marker marker-class-name="section.head.marker">Description</fo:marker>
          <fo:block font-size="12pt" font-weight="bold">Description</fo:block>
        </fo:block>
      </fo:block>
      <fo:block id="id588034">
        <fo:block font-size="14pt" font-weight="bold">
          <fo:block xmlns:d="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook"
            xmlns:l="http://docbook.sourceforge.net/xmlns/l10n/1.0"
            xmlns:rx="http://www.renderx.com/XSL/Extensions" font-family="Helvetica"
            start-indent="0pt" keep-with-next.within-page="always">
            <fo:table table-layout="fixed" width="100%">
              <fo:table-column column-width="20mm" />
              <fo:table-column column-width="proportional-column-width(1)" />
              <fo:table-body>
                <fo:table-row>
                  <fo:table-cell>
                    <fo:block text-align="left">1</fo:block>
                  </fo:table-cell>
                  <fo:table-cell>
                    <fo:block text-align="left">Introduction</fo:block>
                  </fo:table-cell>
                </fo:table-row>
              </fo:table-body>
            </fo:table>
          </fo:block>
        </fo:block>

        <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt" space-before.maximum="12pt">
          <fo:basic-link external-destination="url(http://www.s1000d.org)" color="blue"
            text-decoration="underline">S1000D</fo:basic-link> is an XML based documentation
          standard for technical publications. Originally created by a conglomerate of defence
          companies for documenting military hardware, it is now also being used by civilian
          aerospace companies. <fo:basic-link
            external-destination="url(http://github.com/smartavionics/S1000D-XSL-Stylesheets)"
            color="blue" text-decoration="underline">S1000D XSL Stylesheets</fo:basic-link> is a set
          of XSL stylesheets that can be used to transform an S1000D document (authored as a bunch
          of XML files) into a PDF file for viewing and printing. </fo:block>
        <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt" space-before.maximum="12pt">
          The specification is still evolving and has already has gone through quite a few
          revisions. The current
          released issue is 4.0.1 and 4.1 is due sometime soon. The specification decrees not only
          how a document's
          content is to be structured but also how the resulting publication should look. The fact
          that the current
          issue runs to more than 2750 pages gives an indication of the complexity of the standard.
          Furthermore,
          companies that create S1000D documents can apply company specific "Business Rules" that
          affect both the
          information content and/or presentation style of the resulting document.
        </fo:block>
        <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt" space-before.maximum="12pt">
          S1000D authoring/publishing systems are available but tend to be geared towards
          the larger companies that are producing 10,000's of pages of documentation. So at the
          moment, if you
          want to produce a modest sized S1000D document you have to either invest (rather heavily)
          in an
          authoring/publishing system or pay someone who has the capability to produce S1000D
          documents to create the
          document for you. Either way, it's expensive. The goal of the S1000D XSL stylesheet
          project is to provide a
          low-cost (in terms of the software cost) means to create page-oriented output (PDF files)
          from S1000D
          compliant XML source files.
        </fo:block>
        <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt" space-before.maximum="12pt">
          The S1000D standard describes how the XML <fo:inline font-weight="bold">data modules</fo:inline>
          that make up a given publication's content are stored in a "Common Source DataBase"
          (CSDB). They don't actually say how the database is implemented or how the data modules
          are authored and stored in the database but the basic idea is that when a publication is
          to be created, the required modules are extracted from the database and formatted along
          with some (possibly automatically generated) front matter to produce the desired result.
          The content of a given publication is determined by one or more <fo:inline
            font-weight="bold">publication modules</fo:inline> which reference the data modules to
          be included. </fo:block>
        <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt" space-before.maximum="12pt">
          Commercial S1000D publishing systems provide some form of Content Management
          System (CMS) to act as the CSDB and that would provide versioning and multi-author access
          facilities. For a
          small documentation project, the complicated and expensive CMS can be replaced with a
          decent Software
          Configuration Management (SCM) system and the modules stored simply as flat files. As
          described in more detail
          below, the S1000D XSL stylesheets simply require that a publication's data modules and
          publication module are
          combined to make a single XML file which is then processed - how those modules are stored
          and retrieved is not
          really relevant.
        </fo:block>
      </fo:block>
      <fo:block id="id588069">
        <fo:block font-size="14pt" font-weight="bold">
          <fo:block xmlns:d="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook"
            xmlns:l="http://docbook.sourceforge.net/xmlns/l10n/1.0"
            xmlns:rx="http://www.renderx.com/XSL/Extensions" font-family="Helvetica"
            start-indent="0pt" keep-with-next.within-page="always">
            <fo:table table-layout="fixed" width="100%">
              <fo:table-column column-width="20mm" />
              <fo:table-column column-width="proportional-column-width(1)" />
              <fo:table-body>
                <fo:table-row>
                  <fo:table-cell>
                    <fo:block text-align="left">2</fo:block>
                  </fo:table-cell>
                  <fo:table-cell>
                    <fo:block text-align="left">Using the S1000D XSL stylesheets</fo:block>
                  </fo:table-cell>
                </fo:table-row>
              </fo:table-body>
            </fo:table>
          </fo:block>
        </fo:block>

        <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt" space-before.maximum="12pt">
          To create a PDF file from a publication module and one or more data modules, you need to
          put together a simple top-level XML file that includes all of the desired content within a
          root element. The tag used for the root element can be anything<fo:footnote>
            <fo:inline>
              <fo:inline font-family="Helvetica" font-size="75%" font-weight="normal"
                font-style="normal" baseline-shift="super">1</fo:inline>
            </fo:inline>
            <fo:footnote-body font-family="Helvetica" font-size="7pt" font-weight="normal"
              font-style="normal" text-align="left" start-indent="0pt" text-indent="0pt"
              hyphenate="false" wrap-option="wrap" linefeed-treatment="treat-as-space"
              end-indent="0pt">
              <fo:block><fo:inline font-family="Helvetica" font-size="75%" font-weight="normal"
                  font-style="normal" baseline-shift="super">1</fo:inline> Probably not a good idea
          to use an element name like <fo:inline>
                  <fo:inline font-family="monospace">pm</fo:inline>
                </fo:inline> or <fo:inline>
                  <fo:inline font-family="monospace">dmodule</fo:inline>
                </fo:inline>
          .
          </fo:block>
            </fo:footnote-body>
          </fo:footnote>, I am using <fo:inline>
            <fo:inline font-family="monospace">publication</fo:inline>
          </fo:inline>. Here
          is the top-level file used to create this README: <fo:inline>
            <fo:inline font-family="monospace">&lt;?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?&gt;
          &lt;publication xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude"&gt;
              &lt;xi:include href="PMC-S1000DXSL-SMART-00001-00.xml"/&gt;
              &lt;xi:include
              href="DMC-S1000DXSL-A-00-00-00-00A-040A-A_001-00_EN-GB.xml"/&gt;
              &lt;/publication&gt;</fo:inline>
          </fo:inline>
        </fo:block>
        <fo:block id="id588092" space-before.minimum="0.8em" space-before.optimum="1em"
          space-before.maximum="1.2em" margin-left="0.25in" margin-right="0.25in">
          <fo:block keep-with-next.within-column="always" font-size="12pt" text-align="center"
            font-weight="bold" text-decoration="underline" hyphenate="false">Note</fo:block>
          <fo:block font-size="10pt" text-align="left">
            <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
              space-before.maximum="12pt">
              The order in which the modules are included is not important.
            </fo:block>
          </fo:block>
        </fo:block>
        <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt" space-before.maximum="12pt">
          Generating a PDF file from this top-level XML file is a three stage process: <fo:list-block
            id="id588112" space-before.optimum="1em" space-before.minimum="0.8em"
            space-before.maximum="1.2em" space-after.optimum="1em" space-after.minimum="0.8em"
            space-after.maximum="1.2em" provisional-distance-between-starts="7mm"
            provisional-label-separation="0.2em">
            <fo:list-item id="id588116" space-before.optimum="0.5em" space-before.minimum="0.4em"
              space-before.maximum="0.6em">
              <fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                <fo:block>1.</fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-label>
              <fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                <fo:block>
                  <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                    space-before.maximum="12pt"> Transform the S1000D XML into DocBook XML using the <fo:inline
                      font-weight="bold">s1000dtodb</fo:inline> stylesheet. </fo:block>
                </fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-body>
            </fo:list-item>
            <fo:list-item id="id588164" space-before.optimum="0.5em" space-before.minimum="0.4em"
              space-before.maximum="0.6em">
              <fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                <fo:block>2.</fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-label>
              <fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                <fo:block>
                  <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                    space-before.maximum="12pt"> Transform the DocBook XML into Formatting Objects
          (FO) XML using the <fo:inline font-weight="bold">dbtofo</fo:inline> stylesheet. </fo:block>
                </fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-body>
            </fo:list-item>
            <fo:list-item id="id588163" space-before.optimum="0.5em" space-before.minimum="0.4em"
              space-before.maximum="0.6em">
              <fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                <fo:block>3.</fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-label>
              <fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                <fo:block>
                  <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                    space-before.maximum="12pt"> Process the FO XML using a formatting program such
          as <fo:inline font-weight="bold">fop</fo:inline> or <fo:inline font-weight="bold">xep</fo:inline>
          to produce the PDF file. </fo:block>
                </fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-body>
            </fo:list-item>
          </fo:list-block>
          An XSLT version 1 compatible processor is required to carry out the transformations. The
          stylesheets have been tested with the <fo:inline font-weight="bold">xalan</fo:inline> and <fo:inline
            font-weight="bold">saxonpe</fo:inline> processors. An <fo:inline font-weight="bold">ant</fo:inline>
          build file is provided that will use either of those programs to do the transformations
          and either fop or xep to do the formatting. Once this process has been configured, the
          document can be rebuilt anytime by simply running ant. </fo:block>
      </fo:block>
      <fo:block id="id588178">
        <fo:block font-size="14pt" font-weight="bold">
          <fo:block xmlns:d="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook"
            xmlns:l="http://docbook.sourceforge.net/xmlns/l10n/1.0"
            xmlns:rx="http://www.renderx.com/XSL/Extensions" font-family="Helvetica"
            start-indent="0pt" keep-with-next.within-page="always">
            <fo:table table-layout="fixed" width="100%">
              <fo:table-column column-width="20mm" />
              <fo:table-column column-width="proportional-column-width(1)" />
              <fo:table-body>
                <fo:table-row>
                  <fo:table-cell>
                    <fo:block text-align="left">3</fo:block>
                  </fo:table-cell>
                  <fo:table-cell>
                    <fo:block text-align="left">Requirements</fo:block>
                  </fo:table-cell>
                </fo:table-row>
              </fo:table-body>
            </fo:table>
          </fo:block>
        </fo:block>

        <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt" space-before.maximum="12pt">
          To use the S1000D XSL stylesheets, you need the following items: <fo:list-block
            id="id588173" provisional-distance-between-starts="50mm+1em"
            provisional-label-separation="1em" space-before.optimum="1em"
            space-before.minimum="0.8em" space-before.maximum="1.2em" space-after.optimum="1em"
            space-after.minimum="0.8em" space-after.maximum="1.2em">
            <fo:list-item id="id588195" space-before.optimum="0.5em" space-before.minimum="0.4em"
              space-before.maximum="0.6em">
              <fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()" text-align="start">
                <fo:block>
                  <fo:inline>S1000D XSL stylesheets</fo:inline>
                </fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-label>
              <fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                <fo:block>
                  <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                    space-before.maximum="12pt">Obtain from the github <fo:basic-link
                      external-destination="url(https://github.com/smartavionics/S1000D-XSL-Stylesheets)"
                      color="blue" text-decoration="underline">S1000D-XSL-Stylesheets</fo:basic-link>
          repository. </fo:block>
                  <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                    space-before.maximum="12pt"> The downloads button on the RHS of the github page
          can be used to download an archive of the latest revision. Better still, install <fo:inline
                      font-weight="bold">git</fo:inline> and then clone the repository onto your
          local machine so that the latest version is only a "git pull" away. </fo:block>
                </fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-body>
            </fo:list-item>
            <fo:list-item id="id588236" space-before.optimum="0.5em" space-before.minimum="0.4em"
              space-before.maximum="0.6em">
              <fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()" text-align="start">
                <fo:block>
                  <fo:inline>Docbook XSL stylesheets</fo:inline>
                </fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-label>
              <fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                <fo:block>
                  <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                    space-before.maximum="12pt">Obtain from <fo:basic-link
                      external-destination="url(http://sourceforge.net/projects/docbook/files/docbook-xsl-ns/)"
                      color="blue" text-decoration="underline">SourceForge</fo:basic-link>. </fo:block>
                  <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                    space-before.maximum="12pt">
                    The dbtofo stylesheet is just a customisation layer on top of the standard
          DocBook XSL stylesheets so
                    you need those stylesheets to use dbtofo. Get the namespace aware version, e.g.
          docbook-xsl-ns-1.76.1
                    and unpack the distribution in the top-level directory of the S1000D XSL tree.
                  </fo:block>
                </fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-body>
            </fo:list-item>
            <fo:list-item id="id588231" space-before.optimum="0.5em" space-before.minimum="0.4em"
              space-before.maximum="0.6em">
              <fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()" text-align="start">
                <fo:block>
                  <fo:inline>An XSLT processor</fo:inline>
                </fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-label>
              <fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                <fo:block>
                  <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                    space-before.maximum="12pt"> If you're going the Java route, there's several to
          choose from - known to work are: <fo:list-block id="id588245"
                      provisional-label-separation="0.2em" provisional-distance-between-starts="7mm">
                      <fo:list-item id="id588249" space-before.optimum="0.5em"
                        space-before.minimum="0.4em" space-before.maximum="0.6em">
                        <fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                          <fo:block>–</fo:block>
                        </fo:list-item-label>
                        <fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                          <fo:block>
                            <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                              space-before.maximum="12pt">
                              <fo:basic-link
                                external-destination="url(http://xml.apache.org/xalan-j/)"
                                color="blue" text-decoration="underline">Xalan2</fo:basic-link>
                            </fo:block>
                          </fo:block>
                        </fo:list-item-body>
                      </fo:list-item>
                      <fo:list-item id="id588254" space-before.optimum="0.5em"
                        space-before.minimum="0.4em" space-before.maximum="0.6em">
                        <fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                          <fo:block>–</fo:block>
                        </fo:list-item-label>
                        <fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                          <fo:block>
                            <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                              space-before.maximum="12pt">
                              <fo:basic-link
                                external-destination="url(http://saxon.sourceforge.net/)"
                                color="blue" text-decoration="underline">Saxon HE</fo:basic-link> or <fo:basic-link
                                external-destination="url(http://www.saxonica.com/)" color="blue"
                                text-decoration="underline">Saxon PE &amp; EE</fo:basic-link>
                            </fo:block>
                            <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                              space-before.maximum="12pt"> The open source version (he) of Saxon is
          fine but it doesn't support the XSL extension currently used in the stylesheets to resolve
          graphic file entities. If you use the commercial editions of Saxon (saxon pe, saxon ee)
          then the extension is supported. See <fo:basic-link
                                internal-destination="ID_S1000DXSL-A-00-00-00-00A-040A-A-par-001">
                                <fo:inline color="blue" text-decoration="underline">below</fo:inline>
                              </fo:basic-link>
          for more information on this. </fo:block>
                          </fo:block>
                        </fo:list-item-body>
                      </fo:list-item>
                    </fo:list-block>
                  </fo:block>
                  <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                    space-before.maximum="12pt"> A non-Java XSLT processor that is known to work is <fo:inline
                      font-weight="bold">
                      <fo:basic-link
                        external-destination="url(http://xmlsoft.org/XSLT/xsltproc2.html)"
                        color="blue" text-decoration="underline">xsltproc</fo:basic-link>
                    </fo:inline>
                  </fo:block>
                </fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-body>
            </fo:list-item>
            <fo:list-item id="id588284" space-before.optimum="0.5em" space-before.minimum="0.4em"
              space-before.maximum="0.6em">
              <fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()" text-align="start">
                <fo:block>
                  <fo:inline>Xerces XML Parser</fo:inline>
                </fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-label>
              <fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                <fo:block>
                  <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                    space-before.maximum="12pt">
                    <fo:basic-link external-destination="url(http://xerces.apache.org/)"
                      color="blue" text-decoration="underline">Xerces2 Java</fo:basic-link> is
          required when using either the xalan or saxon XSLT processors. </fo:block>
                </fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-body>
            </fo:list-item>
            <fo:list-item id="id588344" space-before.optimum="0.5em" space-before.minimum="0.4em"
              space-before.maximum="0.6em">
              <fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()" text-align="start">
                <fo:block>
                  <fo:inline>resolver.jar</fo:inline>
                </fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-label>
              <fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                <fo:block>
                  <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                    space-before.maximum="12pt"> Again, only needed if you are using a Java XSLT
          processor. It can be found in <fo:basic-link
                      external-destination="url(http://www.apache.org/dist/xml/commons/xml-commons-resolver-latest.zip)"
                      color="blue" text-decoration="underline">xml-commons-resolver-latest.zip</fo:basic-link>
          .
                </fo:block>
                </fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-body>
            </fo:list-item>
            <fo:list-item id="id588349" space-before.optimum="0.5em" space-before.minimum="0.4em"
              space-before.maximum="0.6em">
              <fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()" text-align="start">
                <fo:block>
                  <fo:inline>FO Processor</fo:inline>
                </fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-label>
              <fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                <fo:block>
                  <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                    space-before.maximum="12pt"> There are numerous commercial products that will
          generate PDF (and other formats) from XML FO input. The stylesheets have been tested with <fo:basic-link
                      external-destination="url(http://www.renderx.com/tools/xep.html)" color="blue"
                      text-decoration="underline">XEP from RenderX</fo:basic-link> product. </fo:block>
                  <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                    space-before.maximum="12pt"> A free alternative is <fo:basic-link
                      external-destination="url(http://xmlgraphics.apache.org/fop/)" color="blue"
                      text-decoration="underline">Apache Fop</fo:basic-link>. Version 1 works with
          the stylesheets. </fo:block>
                </fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-body>
            </fo:list-item>
            <fo:list-item id="id588387" space-before.optimum="0.5em" space-before.minimum="0.4em"
              space-before.maximum="0.6em">
              <fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()" text-align="start">
                <fo:block>
                  <fo:inline>Apache ant</fo:inline>
                </fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-label>
              <fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                <fo:block>
                  <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                    space-before.maximum="12pt"> If you want to use the supplied ant build file, you
          need ant which may well be available as a package on your system or it can be obtained
          from <fo:basic-link external-destination="url(http://ant.apache.org)" color="blue"
                      text-decoration="underline">Apache ant</fo:basic-link>. </fo:block>
                </fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-body>
            </fo:list-item>
          </fo:list-block>
        </fo:block>
        <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt" space-before.maximum="12pt">
          The ant build file needs to know where the various jars (xalan/saxon, xerces, resolver)
          reside so it will make
          configuration easier if those jars are put into the same directory but it's not an
          absolute requirement.
        </fo:block>
      </fo:block>
      <fo:block id="id588404">
        <fo:block font-size="14pt" font-weight="bold">
          <fo:block xmlns:d="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook"
            xmlns:l="http://docbook.sourceforge.net/xmlns/l10n/1.0"
            xmlns:rx="http://www.renderx.com/XSL/Extensions" font-family="Helvetica"
            start-indent="0pt" keep-with-next.within-page="always">
            <fo:table table-layout="fixed" width="100%">
              <fo:table-column column-width="20mm" />
              <fo:table-column column-width="proportional-column-width(1)" />
              <fo:table-body>
                <fo:table-row>
                  <fo:table-cell>
                    <fo:block text-align="left">4</fo:block>
                  </fo:table-cell>
                  <fo:table-cell>
                    <fo:block text-align="left">Configuration</fo:block>
                  </fo:table-cell>
                </fo:table-row>
              </fo:table-body>
            </fo:table>
          </fo:block>
        </fo:block>

        <fo:block id="id588433" space-before.minimum="0.8em" space-before.optimum="1em"
          space-before.maximum="1.2em" margin-left="0.25in" margin-right="0.25in">
          <fo:block keep-with-next.within-column="always" font-size="12pt" text-align="center"
            font-weight="bold" text-decoration="underline" hyphenate="false">Note</fo:block>
          <fo:block font-size="10pt" text-align="left">
            <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
              space-before.maximum="12pt"> This section assumes you are using ant with the supplied <fo:inline
                font-weight="bold">build.xml</fo:inline> file. </fo:block>
          </fo:block>
        </fo:block>
        <fo:block id="id588407">
          <fo:block font-size="12pt" font-weight="bold">
            <fo:block xmlns:d="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook"
              xmlns:l="http://docbook.sourceforge.net/xmlns/l10n/1.0"
              xmlns:rx="http://www.renderx.com/XSL/Extensions" font-family="Helvetica"
              start-indent="0pt" keep-with-next.within-page="always">
              <fo:table table-layout="fixed" width="100%">
                <fo:table-column column-width="20mm" />
                <fo:table-column column-width="proportional-column-width(1)" />
                <fo:table-body>
                  <fo:table-row>
                    <fo:table-cell>
                      <fo:block text-align="left">4.1</fo:block>
                    </fo:table-cell>
                    <fo:table-cell>
                      <fo:block text-align="left">Configuring the build</fo:block>
                    </fo:table-cell>
                  </fo:table-row>
                </fo:table-body>
              </fo:table>
            </fo:block>
          </fo:block>

          <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
            space-before.maximum="12pt"> Configuration mainly consists of setting those build
            parameters that you wish to change from the default values. To do that, don't edit
            build.xml itself but, instead, edit <fo:inline font-weight="bold">build.properties</fo:inline>.
            The most important properties you can set are listed in <fo:basic-link
              internal-destination="ID_S1000DXSL-A-00-00-00-00A-040A-A-tab-001">
              <fo:inline color="blue" text-decoration="underline">Table 2</fo:inline>
            </fo:basic-link>
            .
          </fo:block>
          <fo:block id="ID_S1000DXSL-A-00-00-00-00A-040A-A-tab-001"
            keep-together.within-column="auto" space-before.minimum="0.5em"
            space-before.optimum="1em" space-before.maximum="2em" space-after.minimum="0.5em"
            space-after.optimum="1em" space-after.maximum="2em">
            <fo:block font-style="italic" font-size="10pt" font-weight="normal" text-align="center"
              hyphenate="false" space-after.minimum="0.4em" space-after.optimum="0.6em"
              space-after.maximum="0.8em" space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
              space-before.maximum="12pt" keep-with-next.within-column="always">Table 2
               Configurable build properties</fo:block>
          </fo:block>
        </fo:block>
        <fo:block id="id588814">
          <fo:block font-size="12pt" font-weight="bold">
            <fo:block xmlns:d="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook"
              xmlns:l="http://docbook.sourceforge.net/xmlns/l10n/1.0"
              xmlns:rx="http://www.renderx.com/XSL/Extensions" font-family="Helvetica"
              start-indent="0pt" keep-with-next.within-page="always">
              <fo:table table-layout="fixed" width="100%">
                <fo:table-column column-width="20mm" />
                <fo:table-column column-width="proportional-column-width(1)" />
                <fo:table-body>
                  <fo:table-row>
                    <fo:table-cell>
                      <fo:block text-align="left">4.2</fo:block>
                    </fo:table-cell>
                    <fo:table-cell>
                      <fo:block text-align="left">Catalog configuration</fo:block>
                    </fo:table-cell>
                  </fo:table-row>
                </fo:table-body>
              </fo:table>
            </fo:block>
          </fo:block>

          <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
            space-before.maximum="12pt"> For the XSLT processor to be able to find the DocBook
            stylesheets, the catalog file (<fo:inline font-weight="bold">catalog.xml</fo:inline>) in
            the S1000D XSL installation directory needs to have a suitable entry. The supplied file
            looks like this: <fo:inline>
              <fo:inline font-family="monospace">&lt;?xml version="1.0"?&gt;

                &lt;catalog xmlns="urn:oasis:names:tc:entity:xmlns:xml:catalog"&gt;

                &lt;nextCatalog catalog="docbook-xsl-ns-1.76.1/catalog.xml"/&gt;
                &lt;nextCatalog catalog="../catalog.xml"/&gt;

                &lt;/catalog&gt;</fo:inline>
            </fo:inline> The first <fo:inline
              font-weight="bold">nextCatalog</fo:inline> element should reference the catalog in the
            DocBook XSL distribution. Make sure that the pathname there matches where your DocBook
            XSL files are located. </fo:block>
        </fo:block>
        <fo:block id="ID_S1000DXSL-A-00-00-00-00A-040A-A-par-001">
          <fo:block font-size="12pt" font-weight="bold">
            <fo:block xmlns:d="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook"
              xmlns:l="http://docbook.sourceforge.net/xmlns/l10n/1.0"
              xmlns:rx="http://www.renderx.com/XSL/Extensions" font-family="Helvetica"
              start-indent="0pt" keep-with-next.within-page="always">
              <fo:table table-layout="fixed" width="100%">
                <fo:table-column column-width="20mm" />
                <fo:table-column column-width="proportional-column-width(1)" />
                <fo:table-body>
                  <fo:table-row>
                    <fo:table-cell>
                      <fo:block text-align="left">4.3</fo:block>
                    </fo:table-cell>
                    <fo:table-cell>
                      <fo:block text-align="left">Graphics entity map configuration</fo:block>
                    </fo:table-cell>
                  </fo:table-row>
                </fo:table-body>
              </fo:table>
            </fo:block>
          </fo:block>

          <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
            space-before.maximum="12pt"> S1000D documents reference all graphics through XML
            entities. At some stage in the processing, the entity has to be mapped into a filename
            which is then passed through to the FO processor so it can include the graphic in the
            output. Entities can be defined in a local document type definition at the top of a
            module that uses them. Here's an example: <fo:inline>
              <fo:inline font-family="monospace">&lt;!NOTATION cgm
                PUBLIC "-//USA-DOD//NOTATION Computer Graphics Metafile//EN"&gt;
                &lt;!ENTITY ICN-S1000DBIKE-AAA-DA53000-0-U8025-00525-A-04-1 SYSTEM
            "./illustrations/ICN-S1000DBIKE-AAA-DA53000-0-U8025-00525-A-04-1.CGM"
                NDATA cgm&gt;</fo:inline>
            </fo:inline> The first line declares <fo:inline>
              <fo:inline font-family="monospace">cgm</fo:inline>
            </fo:inline> to be a
            type of external entity and the second line declares <fo:inline>
              <fo:inline font-family="monospace">ICN-S1000DBIKE-AAA-DA53000-0-U8025-00525-A-04-1</fo:inline>
            </fo:inline>
            to be an entity of that type that references a CGM graphics file at location <fo:inline>
              <fo:inline font-family="monospace">
            ./illustrations/ICN-S1000DBIKE-AAA-DA53000-0-U8025-00525-A-04-1.CGM</fo:inline>
            </fo:inline>.
            So now, within the data module content, you can use <fo:inline>
              <fo:inline font-family="monospace">ICN-S1000DBIKE-AAA-DA53000-0-U8025-00525-A-04-1</fo:inline>
            </fo:inline>
            to specify that graphics file. </fo:block>
          <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
            space-before.maximum="12pt">
            The XSLT processor is potentially capable of resolving the entity name into the file
            name but to do so
            it must have seen the declarations shown above and for that to happen, it must be using
            a validating XML
            parser. At this time, the build file is using a non-validating parser and so when the
            XSLT processor
            resolves the graphics entity name it comes out null. Therefore, I have implemented a
            simple XSL extension
            function that is used to resolve the graphics entity names.
          </fo:block>
          <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
            space-before.maximum="12pt"> The way it works is as follows: within the modules that
            wish to refer to a graphics file you declare a suitable entity for the file - the type
            and value of the entity are not important as they will both be ignored when the module
            is processed. Here's the declaration that is used within the PMC for this document that
            declares the entity that gets used as the company logo in the page headers: <fo:inline>
              <fo:inline font-family="monospace">&lt;!NOTATION anything SYSTEM ""&gt;
                &lt;!ENTITY publisher-logo SYSTEM "" NDATA anything&gt;</fo:inline>
            </fo:inline>
            This declares <fo:inline>
              <fo:inline font-family="monospace">publisher-logo</fo:inline>
            </fo:inline>
            to be an entity which we can use within the module to specify a graphics file - it gets
            used like this: <fo:inline>
              <fo:inline font-family="monospace">&lt;logo&gt;
                &lt;symbol infoEntityIdent="publisher-logo" reproductionHeight="12mm"/&gt;
            &lt;/logo&gt;</fo:inline>
            </fo:inline> To achieve the desired result, we
            still have to specify somewhere that <fo:inline>
              <fo:inline font-family="monospace">publisher-logo</fo:inline>
            </fo:inline>
            maps into a particular file name (in this case the file is called <fo:inline>
              <fo:inline font-family="monospace">smartavionics-logo.svg</fo:inline>
            </fo:inline>).
            This mapping of entity names to file names is done with a simple property file called <fo:inline>
              <fo:inline font-family="monospace">info-entity-map.txt</fo:inline>
            </fo:inline>
            that contains one line for each graphics entity you want to resolve. The example file
            looks like this: <fo:inline>
              <fo:inline font-family="monospace">publisher-logo = smartavionics-logo.svg
</fo:inline>
            </fo:inline> If the
            property file cannot be found or an entity doesn't have an entry in the file, the entity
            name is passed through as the file name unchanged, i.e. entity name 'foo' maps into
            'foo'. The DocBook stylesheets will automatically append a default file extension if a
            graphics file name has no extension. The default is '.png' so entity 'foo' will become
            'foo.png'. </fo:block>
          <fo:block id="id588909">
            <fo:block font-size="10pt" font-weight="bold">
              <fo:block xmlns:d="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook"
                xmlns:l="http://docbook.sourceforge.net/xmlns/l10n/1.0"
                xmlns:rx="http://www.renderx.com/XSL/Extensions" font-family="Helvetica"
                start-indent="0pt" keep-with-next.within-page="always">
                <fo:table table-layout="fixed" width="100%">
                  <fo:table-column column-width="20mm" />
                  <fo:table-column column-width="proportional-column-width(1)" />
                  <fo:table-body>
                    <fo:table-row>
                      <fo:table-cell>
                        <fo:block text-align="left">4.3.1</fo:block>
                      </fo:table-cell>
                      <fo:table-cell>
                        <fo:block text-align="left">Caveats</fo:block>
                      </fo:table-cell>
                    </fo:table-row>
                  </fo:table-body>
                </fo:table>
              </fo:block>
            </fo:block>

            <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
              space-before.maximum="12pt">
              <fo:list-block id="id588917" space-before.optimum="1em" space-before.minimum="0.8em"
                space-before.maximum="1.2em" space-after.optimum="1em" space-after.minimum="0.8em"
                space-after.maximum="1.2em" provisional-label-separation="0.2em"
                provisional-distance-between-starts="7mm">
                <fo:list-item id="id588922" space-before.optimum="0.5em"
                  space-before.minimum="0.4em" space-before.maximum="0.6em">
                  <fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                    <fo:block>–</fo:block>
                  </fo:list-item-label>
                  <fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                    <fo:block>
                      <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                        space-before.maximum="12pt">
                        This is all rather experimental and could well change in the future.
                      </fo:block>
                    </fo:block>
                  </fo:list-item-body>
                </fo:list-item>
                <fo:list-item id="id588933" space-before.optimum="0.5em"
                  space-before.minimum="0.4em" space-before.maximum="0.6em">
                  <fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                    <fo:block>–</fo:block>
                  </fo:list-item-label>
                  <fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                    <fo:block>
                      <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                        space-before.maximum="12pt">
                        The S1000D standard specifies that all vector graphic files are in CGM
                        format and all bitmap graphic
                        files are in TIFF format. Unfortunately CGM format is not supported by
                        either of the FO processors I
                        have access to and so they cannot be used directly. Potentially, the
                        publishing system could convert
                        CGM files to, say, SVG or EPS for inclusion in the document but as I don't
                        have any means of
                        editing CGM files anyway this capability is not high on my list of
                        improvements.
                      </fo:block>
                    </fo:block>
                  </fo:list-item-body>
                </fo:list-item>
                <fo:list-item id="id588957" space-before.optimum="0.5em"
                  space-before.minimum="0.4em" space-before.maximum="0.6em">
                  <fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                    <fo:block>–</fo:block>
                  </fo:list-item-label>
                  <fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                    <fo:block>
                      <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                        space-before.maximum="12pt">
                        The Home Edition of the Saxon XSLT processor does not support the above
                        mentioned extension and so
                        the entity name will get passed through unchanged as described above.
                      </fo:block>
                    </fo:block>
                  </fo:list-item-body>
                </fo:list-item>
              </fo:list-block>
            </fo:block>
          </fo:block>
        </fo:block>
      </fo:block>
      <fo:block id="id588950">
        <fo:block font-size="14pt" font-weight="bold">
          <fo:block xmlns:d="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook"
            xmlns:l="http://docbook.sourceforge.net/xmlns/l10n/1.0"
            xmlns:rx="http://www.renderx.com/XSL/Extensions" font-family="Helvetica"
            start-indent="0pt" keep-with-next.within-page="always">
            <fo:table table-layout="fixed" width="100%">
              <fo:table-column column-width="20mm" />
              <fo:table-column column-width="proportional-column-width(1)" />
              <fo:table-body>
                <fo:table-row>
                  <fo:table-cell>
                    <fo:block text-align="left">5</fo:block>
                  </fo:table-cell>
                  <fo:table-cell>
                    <fo:block text-align="left">Cranking the handle</fo:block>
                  </fo:table-cell>
                </fo:table-row>
              </fo:table-body>
            </fo:table>
          </fo:block>
        </fo:block>

        <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt" space-before.maximum="12pt">
          Before processing, you need to check that the input is valid S1000D XML. If the input
          isn't valid, expect big trouble. I strongly recommend using an XML aware editor that has
          validating capability. I am currently using jedit (free) and oxygen (not-free) and many
          others are available<fo:footnote>
            <fo:inline>
              <fo:inline font-family="Helvetica" font-size="75%" font-weight="normal"
                font-style="normal" baseline-shift="super">2</fo:inline>
            </fo:inline>
            <fo:footnote-body font-family="Helvetica" font-size="7pt" font-weight="normal"
              font-style="normal" text-align="left" start-indent="0pt" text-indent="0pt"
              hyphenate="false" wrap-option="wrap" linefeed-treatment="treat-as-space"
              end-indent="0pt">
              <fo:block><fo:inline font-family="Helvetica" font-size="75%" font-weight="normal"
                  font-style="normal" baseline-shift="super">2</fo:inline> Emacs NXML mode would be
          usable if the S1000D xsd schemas were available as rnc schemas but I haven't yet succeeded
          in generating usable rnc files from the xsd files. </fo:block>
            </fo:footnote-body>
          </fo:footnote>
          .
        </fo:block>
        <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt" space-before.maximum="12pt">
          Once the source is validated, it's just a matter of running 'ant' within the directory
          containing the
          build.xml file. You can either do this from a command line or if you are using an editor
          like jedit you can
          run the build from within the editor.
        </fo:block>
        <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt" space-before.maximum="12pt">
          It is normal to get a few messages, here's some typical output (unfortunately, some of the
          lines are long and I have had to wrap them):<fo:inline>
            <fo:inline font-family="monospace">
              Buildfile: build.xml

              s1000d_to_db:

              check.transform.required:

              transform.using.xalan:
              [echo] Transforming S1000DXSL-README.xml to S1000DXSL-README-db.xml

              db_to_fo:

              check.transform.required:

              transform.using.xalan:
              [echo] Transforming S1000DXSL-README-db.xml to S1000DXSL-README.fo
              [java] file:/home/smartavionics/S1000D/S1000D_xsl/docbook-xsl-ns-1.76.1/
          fo/docbook.xsl; Line #318; Column #16; Making portrait pages on A4 paper
              (210mmx297mm)

              fo_to_pdf:

              check.format.required:

              format:

              format.using.fop1:
              [exec] 28-Jan-2011 10:35:48 org.apache.fop.events.LoggingEventListener
              processEvent
              [exec] WARNING: Font "Symbol,normal,700" not found. Substituting with
          "Symbol,normal,400".
              [exec] 28-Jan-2011 10:35:48 org.apache.fop.events.LoggingEventListener
              processEvent
              [exec] WARNING: Font "ZapfDingbats,normal,700" not found. Substituting
              with "ZapfDingbats,normal,400".
              [exec] 28-Jan-2011 10:35:48 org.apache.fop.events.LoggingEventListener
              processEvent
              [exec] WARNING: Line 1 of a paragraph overflows the available area by
              802 millipoints. (See position 286:377)
              [move] Moving 1 file to /home/smartavionics/S1000D/S1000D_xsl/sample

              build:

              BUILD SUCCESSFUL
              Total time: 18 seconds
</fo:inline>
          </fo:inline>
          If you use an S1000D element that is not yet implemented by the stylesheets you will get a
          message like this:<fo:inline>
            <fo:inline font-family="monospace">Unhandled: publication/dmodule/content/faultReporting</fo:inline>
          </fo:inline>
          and the element and its content will be copied through to the output verbatim and
          displayed in red. </fo:block>
      </fo:block>
      <fo:block id="id588998">
        <fo:block font-size="14pt" font-weight="bold">
          <fo:block xmlns:d="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook"
            xmlns:l="http://docbook.sourceforge.net/xmlns/l10n/1.0"
            xmlns:rx="http://www.renderx.com/XSL/Extensions" font-family="Helvetica"
            start-indent="0pt" keep-with-next.within-page="always">
            <fo:table table-layout="fixed" width="100%">
              <fo:table-column column-width="20mm" />
              <fo:table-column column-width="proportional-column-width(1)" />
              <fo:table-body>
                <fo:table-row>
                  <fo:table-cell>
                    <fo:block text-align="left">6</fo:block>
                  </fo:table-cell>
                  <fo:table-cell>
                    <fo:block text-align="left">Future work</fo:block>
                  </fo:table-cell>
                </fo:table-row>
              </fo:table-body>
            </fo:table>
          </fo:block>
        </fo:block>

        <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt" space-before.maximum="12pt">
          There's still much to do: <fo:list-block id="id588994" space-before.optimum="1em"
            space-before.minimum="0.8em" space-before.maximum="1.2em" space-after.optimum="1em"
            space-after.minimum="0.8em" space-after.maximum="1.2em"
            provisional-label-separation="0.2em" provisional-distance-between-starts="7mm">
            <fo:list-item id="id588980" space-before.optimum="0.5em" space-before.minimum="0.4em"
              space-before.maximum="0.6em">
              <fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                <fo:block>–</fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-label>
              <fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                <fo:block>
                  <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                    space-before.maximum="12pt">
                    Many elements in the S1000D schema are not yet implemented. I will be
          implementing
                    elements as and when I need them but if you want to use these stylesheets and
          need particular elements
                    implementing, let me know. Better still, if you know some XSL, have a go at
          implementing the elements
                    yourself (all contributions are welcome).
                  </fo:block>
                </fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-body>
            </fo:list-item>
            <fo:list-item id="id589009" space-before.optimum="0.5em" space-before.minimum="0.4em"
              space-before.maximum="0.6em">
              <fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                <fo:block>–</fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-label>
              <fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                <fo:block>
                  <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                    space-before.maximum="12pt">
                    No attempt has been made to support 'applicability'. I believe that the 4.1
          issue of S1000D will have
                    something to say re the formatting aspects of applicability so I am holding off
          for a while.
                  </fo:block>
                </fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-body>
            </fo:list-item>
            <fo:list-item id="id589031" space-before.optimum="0.5em" space-before.minimum="0.4em"
              space-before.maximum="0.6em">
              <fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                <fo:block>–</fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-label>
              <fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                <fo:block>
                  <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                    space-before.maximum="12pt">
                    At the present time, the generation of a title page is problematic. S1000D does
          not currently support
                    any markup to explicitly lay out the elements of a title page. I believe that
          this may be addressed in
                    4.1 but in the meantime, one can "cheat" by directly embedding either FO or
          DocBook elements in the
                    S1000D modules and they will get passed through (thanks to the power of XML
          namespaces!)
                  </fo:block>
                </fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-body>
            </fo:list-item>
            <fo:list-item id="id589013" space-before.optimum="0.5em" space-before.minimum="0.4em"
              space-before.maximum="0.6em">
              <fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                <fo:block>–</fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-label>
              <fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                <fo:block>
                  <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                    space-before.maximum="12pt"> At this time, if the document contains a "List of
          effective data modules" module (indicated by the module having an <fo:inline>
                      <fo:inline font-family="monospace">infoCode</fo:inline>
                    </fo:inline>
          value of <fo:inline>
                      <fo:inline font-family="monospace">00S</fo:inline>
                    </fo:inline>),
          the stylesheets will automatically generate the content for the module from the data
          modules that are referenced by the PM. In the future, other front-matter may well be
          automatically generated. </fo:block>
                </fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-body>
            </fo:list-item>
          </fo:list-block>
        </fo:block>
      </fo:block>
      <fo:block id="id589022">
        <fo:block font-size="14pt" font-weight="bold">
          <fo:block xmlns:d="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook"
            xmlns:l="http://docbook.sourceforge.net/xmlns/l10n/1.0"
            xmlns:rx="http://www.renderx.com/XSL/Extensions" font-family="Helvetica"
            start-indent="0pt" keep-with-next.within-page="always">
            <fo:table table-layout="fixed" width="100%">
              <fo:table-column column-width="20mm" />
              <fo:table-column column-width="proportional-column-width(1)" />
              <fo:table-body>
                <fo:table-row>
                  <fo:table-cell>
                    <fo:block text-align="left">7</fo:block>
                  </fo:table-cell>
                  <fo:table-cell>
                    <fo:block text-align="left">Acknowledgements</fo:block>
                  </fo:table-cell>
                </fo:table-row>
              </fo:table-body>
            </fo:table>
          </fo:block>
        </fo:block>

        <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt" space-before.maximum="12pt">
          This project has been made much easier by the efforts of other people and I would like to
          say thanks to: <fo:list-block id="id589030" space-before.optimum="1em"
            space-before.minimum="0.8em" space-before.maximum="1.2em" space-after.optimum="1em"
            space-after.minimum="0.8em" space-after.maximum="1.2em"
            provisional-label-separation="0.2em" provisional-distance-between-starts="7mm">
            <fo:list-item id="id589061" space-before.optimum="0.5em" space-before.minimum="0.4em"
              space-before.maximum="0.6em">
              <fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                <fo:block>–</fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-label>
              <fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                <fo:block>
                  <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                    space-before.maximum="12pt">
                    The creators/maintainers of the DocBook XSL stylesheets and the associated
          documentation - not only have
                    I used those stylesheets as the basis of the formatting process but I have also
          learnt a lot about XSL
                    stylesheet writing by looking at that code.
                  </fo:block>
                </fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-body>
            </fo:list-item>
            <fo:list-item id="id589070" space-before.optimum="0.5em" space-before.minimum="0.4em"
              space-before.maximum="0.6em">
              <fo:list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                <fo:block>–</fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-label>
              <fo:list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                <fo:block>
                  <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt"
                    space-before.maximum="12pt">
                    The creators/maintainers of the various open source tools that are used (xalan,
          xerces, saxonhe, fop,
                    etc.)
                  </fo:block>
                </fo:block>
              </fo:list-item-body>
            </fo:list-item>
          </fo:list-block>
        </fo:block>
      </fo:block>
      <fo:block id="id589065">
        <fo:block font-size="14pt" font-weight="bold">
          <fo:block xmlns:d="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook"
            xmlns:l="http://docbook.sourceforge.net/xmlns/l10n/1.0"
            xmlns:rx="http://www.renderx.com/XSL/Extensions" font-family="Helvetica"
            start-indent="0pt" keep-with-next.within-page="always">
            <fo:table table-layout="fixed" width="100%">
              <fo:table-column column-width="20mm" />
              <fo:table-column column-width="proportional-column-width(1)" />
              <fo:table-body>
                <fo:table-row>
                  <fo:table-cell>
                    <fo:block text-align="left">8</fo:block>
                  </fo:table-cell>
                  <fo:table-cell>
                    <fo:block text-align="left">Warranty</fo:block>
                  </fo:table-cell>
                </fo:table-row>
              </fo:table-body>
            </fo:table>
          </fo:block>
        </fo:block>

        <fo:block space-before.optimum="10pt" space-before.minimum="8pt" space-before.maximum="12pt">
          The file COPYING contains the full warranty and copyright information but the bottom line
          is that these
          stylesheets (and any other files in this package) are supplied with no warranty as to
          fitness of purpose, etc.
          In particular, the stylesheets are not guaranteed to produce output that is free from
          errors or omissions. So
          if you write a document that states something like "ensure that the fuel tank contains at
          least 10,000L of
          fuel" and it comes out saying "ensure that the fuel tank contains at least 10L of fuel",
          Smart Avionics Ltd.
          will not be liable for the consequences.
        </fo:block>
      </fo:block>
    </fo:flow>
  </fo:page-sequence>
</fo:root>